  "evidence": "TEXT_D: £12bn extra for the NHS, £1.2bn for social care, 8,000 more GPs/20,000 nurses/3,000 midwives; five years’ contributions before full benefits/NHS (beyond urgent care); five‑year moratorium on unskilled immigration; revoke citizenship for terrorists; leave the ECHR; restrict social housing/Help to Buy to citizens or 5‑year contributors; cut aid to 0.2% GNI; leave the EU; proportional voting; replace Barnett with a needs‑based system. TEXT_E: At least £8bn extra for the NHS by 2020, a 7‑day NHS, mental health parity, integrate health and social care, cap residential care costs; 30 hours free childcare, protect per‑pupil funding, free entry to national museums; maintain 0.7% aid, strengthen flood defences and the Blue Belt; controlled immigration via enforcement and welfare rule reforms; ‘make work pay’ with a Tax‑Free Minimum Wage law.",
  "reasoning": "Because care and equality are the top priorities in the profile, TEXT_E’s emphasis on NHS expansion with mental‑health parity, integrated social care, free childcare, protected school funding, free museums, and maintaining 0.7% aid aligns better with broad, inclusive care and equal access. TEXT_D, while investing heavily in the NHS, places greater weight on loyalty/authority cues and out‑group exclusions (strict immigration/benefit access limits, leaving ECHR/EU, aid cuts), which conflicts more with the profile’s high care and equality weights.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "TEXT_E"
}
